    "body": "`BanMan::Ban` computes `nBanUntil = GetTime() + ban_time_offset` (banman.cpp:140) with no range check. The offset comes from the `bantime` argument of `setban` or from `-bantime`, and neither has an upper bound.\n\n```\nsetban 1.2.3.5 add 9223372036854775807\n```\n\nreturns success, but the sum overflows int64. UBSan reports:\n\n```\nsrc/banman.cpp:140:73: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 1790412558 + 9223372036854775807 cannot be represented in type 'int64_t'\n```\n\nWithout UBSan the result wraps to a negative `nBanUntil`, the ban is treated as already expired, and `listbanned` is empty. With `-bantime=9223372036854775807`, every `setban add` without an explicit time is dropped the same way. Values up to `INT64_MAX - now` work, so only the very top of the range is affected. The fuzz target already caps the offset for this reason (fuzz/banman.cpp:26), but the RPC and config inputs were never bounded.\n\nThis uses `SaturatingAdd`, so a huge relative ban time is capped at `INT64_MAX` instead of wrapping. The absolute (`absolute=true`) path is unchanged.\n\nThe added case in `rpc_setban.py` bans with a bantime of `INT64_MAX`, restarts with `-bantime=INT64_MAX`, and checks both bans are listed. It fails on master and passes with the fix. `p2p_disconnect_ban.py` still passes.\n\nAI tools were used to help find this issue and to prepare the patch and test.\n",
